Purpose: Document the NaN payload relaxation

[49m[38;5;10m+     1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # NaN payload: the one place "byte-identical" is not well-defined
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Everything in this crate is compared **bit-for-bit** against the C library
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`f32::to_bits`, no epsilons) with exactly one documented relaxation: when both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C result and the Rust result are NaN, the NaN *sign bit and payload* are not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m compared (`common::canon_f32` maps every NaN to `0x7FC00000`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Why
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m On x86, `mulss`/`addss`/`subss` propagate the **destination** operand when both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m operands are NaN. Which source ends up in the destination register is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instruction selection, not language semantics — and GCC changes its mind
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m depending on the optimisation level, for the *same* C source:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m float c2Dot(c2v a, c2v b) { return a.x*b.x + a.y*b.y; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | build | `c2Dot((+NaN,+NaN), (-NaN,-NaN))` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |-------|-----------------------------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `gcc -O0` | `0xffc00000` (destination = right operand) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `gcc -O1`/`-O2`/`-O3` | `0x7fc00000` (destination = left operand) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `rustc` (debug and release) | `0x7fc00000` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduction: `gcc -O0` emits `mulss %xmm1,%xmm0` with the *right* factor in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%xmm0`; `gcc -O1`+ and LLVM emit it with the *left* factor in `%xmm0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust translation therefore already matches an optimised build of the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m source exactly. "Fixing" it to match `-O0` would mean writing every product and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sum with its operands reversed (`b.x*a.x + ...`), which is (a) numerically
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m identical for all non-NaN inputs anyway, and (b) would then *diverge* from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `gcc -O1`+ builds of the identical C. That trades a defined mismatch for an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m equally arbitrary one, so it is deliberately not done.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What is still compared strictly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every NaN case whose result is independent of operand order is compared with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m full bit equality (`common::same_strict`), and is covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/error_paths.rs::err_nonfinite_scalar_helpers` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/nan_strict.rs`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * a single NaN operand (the NaN is propagated regardless of which register is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the destination);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `inf - inf`, `inf + (-inf)` → `0xffc00000` in both;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `0 * inf` → `0xffc00000` in both;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `sqrtf(negative)` → `0xffc00000` in both;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `±0`, subnormals, overflow to `±inf`, `FLT_MAX`, `FLT_EPSILON` — all exact.
